<header class="sg-head"><p class="featured-region">Sacred · date unknown · South &amp; Southeast Asia</p><h1 class="sg-title">Mahishasura Mardini Stotra</h1><p class="sg-local"><bdi lang="sa">महिषासुरमर्दिनीस्तोत्रम्</bdi> / <bdi lang="sa-Latn">Mahiṣāsuramardinīstotraṃ</bdi></p><p class="sg-kid" data-say>A Sanskrit hymn of 21 verses praising the Hindu goddess Durga, who in legend defeats the buffalo demon Mahishasura.</p></header>
<dl class="sg-facts"><div><dt>Words</dt><dd>attributed to Adi Shankara</dd></div><div><dt>Music</dt><dd>traditional</dd></div><div><dt>Culture</dt><dd>Hindus, worshippers of the goddess Durga</dd></div><div><dt>Language</dt><dd>Sanskrit</dd></div><div><dt>Date</dt><dd>traditional; attributed to Adi Shankara</dd></div><div><dt>Type</dt><dd>Sacred</dd></div><div><dt>The words</dt><dd>Public domain (traditional Sanskrit hymn, attributed to Adi Shankara)</dd></div></dl>
<div class="sg-body">
<section class="sg-sec" id="story"><h2>The story</h2><p>The Mahishasura Mardini Stotra is a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindu</a> stotra, a hymn of praise, of 21 verses. Its name comes from an epithet of the goddess <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/durga">Durga</a>, &quot;the slayer of the demon Mahisha&quot;. It praises Durga, a principal aspect of the supreme goddess Mahadevi, and her killing of the asura Mahishasura.</p><p>Its authorship is attributed to the theologian Adi Shankara. The hymn is mentioned in the 53rd chapter of the first part of the Shivarahasya Purana.</p><p>It draws on the Devi Mahatmya and refers to several of the goddess's legends: the slaying of Mahishasura, of Raktabija, and of Chanda and Munda. In the Devi Mahatmya's account of Mahishasura's slaying, the asuras under Mahishasura drove the devas out of Svarga, and in anger the gods <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brahma">Brahma</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vishnu">Vishnu</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shiva">Shiva</a>, the Trimurti, joined their energies, which took the form of the goddess <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/durga">Durga</a>. Armed with the gods' weapons, she killed the shape-shifting Mahishasura, who took the forms of a lion, an elephant, a buffalo and finally a man. The gods glorified her as the first being and the origin of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vedas">Vedas</a>, and she promised to save them whenever they were in danger.</p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="meaning"><h2>What it says</h2><p>A hymn of praise to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/durga">Durga</a>. Verse after verse it hails her titles and attributes and celebrates her victories over the demons, returning each time to a cry of victory to the slayer of Mahishasura. The mood is joyful and exultant.</p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="words"><h2>The words</h2><p class="sg-nowords" data-say>The words of this song are in the public domain, but no free, reliable edition of them has been found yet to import them from, and the Institute never types a song&#8217;s words from memory. &#8220;What it says&#8221; above tells what they are about.</p></section>
</div>
